WEBVTT

0
00:00.210 --> 00:01.043
<v Krista>In this video,</v>

1
00:01.043 --> 00:02.760
we're talking about identity properties

2
00:02.760 --> 00:05.260
or what we call the identity numbers.

3
00:05.260 --> 00:08.090
There's a specific identity number for addition,

4
00:08.090 --> 00:11.050
and there's a different identity number for multiplication.

5
00:11.050 --> 00:14.180
The identity number for addition is zero.

6
00:14.180 --> 00:18.620
So for addition, the identity number is zero.

7
00:18.620 --> 00:23.540
For multiplication, the identity number is one.

8
00:23.540 --> 00:25.960
And we call these the identity number

9
00:25.960 --> 00:28.450
because we can always add zero to something,

10
00:28.450 --> 00:30.010
and it will keep its identity.

11
00:30.010 --> 00:32.010
In other words, its value won't change.

12
00:32.010 --> 00:33.810
We can always multiply by one,

13
00:33.810 --> 00:36.090
and whatever we're multiplying won't change.

14
00:36.090 --> 00:37.300
It'll keep its identity.

15
00:37.300 --> 00:40.120
So in other words, these numbers have no effect

16
00:40.120 --> 00:42.440
on the identity when we're adding zero

17
00:42.440 --> 00:44.130
or when we're multiplying one.

18
00:44.130 --> 00:46.611
So for example, if we want to simplify these expressions,

19
00:46.611 --> 00:50.110
x plus zero is still going to be x.

20
00:50.110 --> 00:53.770
This x keeps its identity even though we've added zero.

21
00:53.770 --> 00:56.820
x multiplied by one is still x.

22
00:56.820 --> 00:58.964
So even though we multiplied by one,

23
00:58.964 --> 01:01.380
x still keeps its identity.

24
01:01.380 --> 01:04.080
Adding zero and multiplying by one don't change

25
01:04.080 --> 01:07.700
the identity, so zero is the identity number for addition,

26
01:07.700 --> 01:10.370
and one is the identity number for multiplication.

27
01:10.370 --> 01:13.040
We can do this with small numbers, four plus zero

28
01:13.040 --> 01:16.740
is still four, four times one is still four.

29
01:16.740 --> 01:18.236
And we can do it with big numbers,

30
01:18.236 --> 01:21.693
1,000 plus zero is still 1,000,

31
01:21.693 --> 01:25.388
1,000 times one is still 1,000.

32
01:25.388 --> 01:27.290
We can also do it with variables.

33
01:27.290 --> 01:28.123
So it doesn't matter

34
01:28.123 --> 01:30.120
what we apply the identity numbers to,

35
01:30.120 --> 01:31.472
we're still going to keep the identity

36
01:31.472 --> 01:32.890
of the original value.

37
01:32.890 --> 01:37.280
So xyz plus zero is still xyz.

38
01:37.280 --> 01:40.967
And xyz times one is still xyz.

39
01:42.540 --> 01:45.140
So the identity number for addition is zero,

40
01:45.140 --> 01:47.773
the identity number for multiplication is one.